<title>SWITZERLAND: ACI says Memphis top world cargo airport in Oct '96.</title>
<headline>ACI says Memphis top world cargo airport in Oct '96.</headline>
<dateline>GENEVA 1997-01-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>Memphis, base of U.S. express freight carrier, Federal Express, was the world's busiest cargo airport in October 1996, said Geneva-based Airports Council International (ACI).</p>
<p>The airport handled 182,720 tonnes of cargo in the month in question, up 24.8 percent on October 1995.</p>
<p>Occupying second-place in the ACI table of top cargo-handling airports was Chicago's O'Hare airport as a result of reporting 167,320 tonnes, up 55.0 percent on the previous year's levels.</p>
<p>In third place was Miami which handled 163,012 tonnes, an increase of 14.0 percent on October 1995.</p>
<p>The world's 20 busiest cargo airports in October 1996, with the volume they handled, the percentage change over October 1995 and, in parentheses, their October 1995 ranking, were:</p>
<p> 1  (1)  Memphis		182,720 tonnes	(up 24.8 pct)</p>
<p> 2  (10) Chicago		167,320 tonnes	(up 55.0)</p>
<p> 3  (3)  Miami		  163,012 tonnes	(up 14.0)</p>
<p> 4  (4)  Los Angeles	  161,706 tonnes	(up 13.6)</p>
<p> 5  (5)  Hong Kong	    156,642 tonnes	(up 10.2)</p>
<p> 6  (2)  Tokyo-Narita	 154,262 tonnes	(up  4.6)</p>
<p> 7  (6)  New York	     154,055 tonnes	(up  5.4)</p>
<p> 8  (7)  Frankfurt	    136,555 tonnes	(up  4.0)</p>
<p> 9  (9)  Seoul		  130,644 tonnes	(up 16.0)</p>
<p> 10 (8)  Louisville	   123,967 tonnes	(up  7.7)</p>
<p> 11 (14) Anchorage	    118,999 tonnes	(up 32.6)</p>
<p> 12 (12) Singapore	    111,488 tonnes	(up 13.4)</p>
<p> 13 (11) London Heathrow    103,473 tonnes	(up  1.3)</p>
<p> 14 (13) Amsterdam	    102,795 tonnes	(up 14.2)</p>
<p> 15 (16) New York Newark     89,184 tonnes	(up 10.8)</p>
<p> 16 (15) Paris-CDG	     86,392 tonnes	(up  4.2)</p>
<p> 17 (19) Taipei		  76,404 tonnes	(up 15.7)</p>
<p> 18 (17) Dallas Fort Worth   75,845 tonnes    (down  3.8)</p>
<p> 19 (18) Atlanta		 75,085 tonnes	(up 13.4)</p>
<p> 20 (-)  Dayton		  70,118 tonnes	(up 23.0)</p>
